# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there. MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST carry --swa-full —
# without it every Claude Code turn re-processes the whole prompt.
MODEL_TITLE=Coding agent + vision + judge · 16.7 GiB · production since 25.08.
MODEL_SWA=no
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched, so a sharded quant comes down whole and an
# mmproj rides along. Read by setup/get-model.sh, which is this stack's
# equivalent of `ollama pull` — except that the list it offers is MEASURED on
# this hardware (bash setup/lib/models.sh table) rather than a generic catalog.
# Verified 26.08. by matching the exact filename in the repo, not by guessing.
MODEL_SOURCE=unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF UD-Q4_K_XL mmproj-F16
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — what one token of context costs in KV cache,
# MEASURED. Read by setup/lib/budget.py, which refuses a start that would not
# fit before it can freeze the machine.
#
# It is DECLARED rather than derived, and this model is the reason. The plain
# architecture arithmetic (65 layers x 4 KV heads x 512 x 2 B) gives 256 KiB
# per token; the measured figure is a quarter of that, because qwen38 is a
# hybrid — only about every fourth layer keeps a full KV cache, the rest carry
# a small recurrent state. A guard that derived its own number would refuse
# the one profile on this machine that is known to fit.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=74.3
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=27.08.2026, from the saved prefixes at a large context. The 25.08. figure taken from the slot states (2.81 GiB for 43,671 tokens) is 67.5; the higher of two measurements is the one to guard with.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Coding agent + vision + judge · 16.7 GiB of weights · measured champion
# of the 24./25.08. model decision (docs/MODELS.md): 9/9 on the task
# battery vs Laguna's 8/9, whole battery faster, and the only model in the
# house that solved the long-context computation.
#
# KV cost MEASURED, not estimated (25.08., from the saved slot states:
#   2.81 GiB for 43,671 tokens): ~67.5 KiB per token in f16. The pure
#   architecture arithmetic (65 layers x 4 KV heads x 512 x 2 B = 256 KiB)
#   is four times that — qwen35 is a HYBRID: only about every fourth layer
#   keeps a full KV cache, the rest carry a small recurrent state. That is
#   what makes a large window affordable here at all.
# -c 409600 with -np 2 --no-kv-unified: 204,800 tokens per slot, i.e. the
#   200k the operator asked for, matching CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS
#   =200000 in setup/claude/local.json.
#
# -np 1 AND a patched binary. TWO independent defects force this; both are
# measured, and the matrix is the whole argument (bench/suites/np2-candidates.py
# and bench/suites/slot-corruption.py, fresh server per cell):
#
#     build     slots  saved prefixes   result
#     stock       2        empty        CORRUPT 6/6
#     patched     2        empty        clean ... then CORRUPT 3/6 on the
#                                       next start, same config
#     patched     2      populated      CORRUPT 4/4 (restores in the log)
#     stock       1      populated      clean
#     patched     1      populated      clean, restore path included
#
#   1) The gfx1151 HIP race. The stock build corrupts with two slots even
#      with an empty store. setup/patches/hip-integrated-off.patch turns
#      off the one flag responsible — the same flag upstream already
#      disables for CUDA "due to issues with corrupted output" (llama.cpp
#      #27579, #27506). Cost, measured on identical bodies: prefill -6.0 %,
#      decode unchanged.
#   2) Slot restore. With two slots, restoring a saved prefix poisons the
#      context: the log shows RESTORED and every answer afterwards is
#      '////'. A consumer on a second machine saw the milder form of the
#      same thing — a coherent answer from ANOTHER session, six times.
#
#   The patch alone is NOT enough: patched + two slots was clean on one
#   start and corrupt on the next, so two slots are a gamble per server
#   start on this hardware. One slot was clean in every cell, restores
#   included. Correctness beats aggregate throughput.
#
#   The price: Claude Code sends TWO prompt types (main request and title
#   generation) which now share one slot and evict each other. -cram keeps
#   the evicted prefix in RAM, measured by the consumer as ~4 s instead of
#   a ~50 s re-prefill.
#
# REMEASURED 26.08. ON b10631 — and the first defect no longer reproduces.
#   bench/suites/np2-candidates.py rocm-patched+cram+mmproj, FOUR fresh
#   server starts, 24 of 24 answers clean. On b10577 the same cell was clean
#   on one start and CORRUPT 3/6 on the next. llama.cpp #27572 and #27579 are
#   both still open, so this is an upstream change nobody announced.
#
#   -np 1 STAYS, for three reasons that the measurement does not touch:
#     * the cell runs -c 32768; production runs -c 204800, and the race is
#       timing-dependent;
#     * it says nothing about defect 2, the slot RESTORE with two slots,
#       which was CORRUPT 4/4 with a populated store — see
#       a restore-path measurement at two slots would, and nobody has
#       written one;
#     * the failure is intermittent and SILENT. Four clean starts raise the
#       odds, they do not make an argument.
#   What it does mean: the question is worth reopening, with the restore path
#   measured, and not on the day a new model lands.
#
#   Rebuild the patched binary after every llama.cpp update:
#     bash setup/scripts/build-llama.sh --activate
#
# WHAT THE STOCK BUILD DOES WITH TWO SLOTS — kept because it explains all this:
#   Isolated with two prefixes alternating; no concurrency needed. The
#   moment a second slot is used, every later answer degenerates to '////'
#   until the server restarts. Measured (bench/suites/slot-corruption.py):
#       one prefix, sequential       clean
#       two prefixes, sequential     CORRUPT on the 2nd request
#       two prefixes, parallel       CORRUPT 8/8
#       all of the above with -np 1  clean
#   Neither concurrency (serialising in the gateway did not help) nor the
#   RAM cache (disabling it did not help) nor slot restore: it is the
#   gfx1151 HIP race, llama.cpp #27579 ("triggers from --parallel >= 2"),
#   root cause in #27572. Not fixable in this repo.
#   The price: Claude Code sends TWO prompt types (main request and title
#   generation) which now share one slot and evict each other. -cram keeps
#   the evicted one in RAM, which is what makes that affordable.
#
# -c 204800: the 200k window, now in a single slot. KV costs 204,800 x
#   67.5 KiB = 13.2 GiB. It had to grow because a real Claude Code session
#   on the second machine sent 78,826 tokens and was rejected ("exceeds the
#   available context size") — its tool head alone is ~43k.
# -cram 32768: RAM-backed prompt cache. Suspected of the corruption on
#   26.08. and exonerated (disabling it changed nothing); with a single
#   slot it earns its keep, because an evicted prefix comes back from RAM
#   instead of being prefilled again.
# f16 KV is deliberate: the one q8_0-KV cell measured (24.08.,
#   rocm-medium-spec-q8kv) dropped the long-context task that f16 solved.
#   No SWA in this model, so no --swa-full needed.
# NOTE on depth: a window is capacity, not speed — but what fills it costs.
#   Decode reads weights PLUS the filled KV per token, so ~10 t/s at 43k
#   and ~5-7 t/s at 200k. Deep sessions are slow by physics, in any
#   configuration. And an auto-saved state grows with the session depth
#   (~13 GiB at 200k) — AUTO_MAX_GB in the gateway env is what bounds that
#   until saving only the system head is built, which nobody has built.
# -ub 2048: prefill batch, measured ~216 t/s cold on ROCm.
# Speculation (MTP head is inside this GGUF, nextn_predict_layers=1):
#   draft-mtp + ngram-mod. Measured decode: code 20-41 t/s, warm multi-turn
#   edits up to 87 t/s, free prose ~8-12 (spec cannot help there).
#
#   IT USED TO COST ONE ANSWER PER TURN — fixed 30.08.2026 by
#   setup/patches/speculation-stops-at-eog.patch, which this build carries.
#   The story is worth keeping because the patch can go missing: see
#   `the-previous-answer-is-sometimes-not-reused` in
#   setup/defects.json. An accepted draft token is left in the slot past the
#   end of a turn, the slot stops being a prefix of the next prompt, and
#   because this model is HYBRID it cannot be trimmed — llama.cpp rolls back
#   to a checkpoint from before the answer and re-prefills the whole thing.
#   A/B/A on 30.08.2026: with these flags TAIL 2 in 9 of 9 rounds, without
#   them TAIL 0 in 7 of 7 and every answer kept.
#
#   THE FLAGS WOULD HAVE STAYED ON EVEN UNFIXED, and the arithmetic is why
#   — kept because it is what to fall back on if the patch is ever lost. Controlled the same
#   evening (same prompt, n_predict 200, three rounds each):
#     code   20.89 t/s with, 9.06 without    prose  8.52 with, 9.09 without
#   The re-prefill is paid at ~199 t/s, so it is 10.5 % of generation time on
#   code — against a 2.3x decode. Keeping speculation is 2.09x faster IN
#   SPITE of the defect. On prose alone the trade would flip, which is why
#   this belongs to THIS profile and is not a rule for every model here.
#   Removing all three flags is the switch, and there is no per-request one:
#   `speculative.type` is dropped by every route.
#
#   AND IT IS draft-mtp SPECIFICALLY, measured one type at a time on the same
#   evening — which also settles what ngram-mod is contributing:
#       spec-type            TAIL        code t/s   prose t/s   (n=3 each,
#                                                                see caveat)
#       draft-mtp,ngram-mod  2           20.89       8.52     <- production
#       draft-mtp            2 (2 of 4)  21.68       8.67
#       ngram-mod            0 (4 of 4)   9.28       8.97
#       none                 0 (7 of 7)   9.06       9.09
#   CAVEAT ON THOSE RATES, measured 30.08.2026: decode on this machine has a
#   19 % coefficient of variation (seven identical rounds: 15.4 to 27.5 t/s).
#   A three-round mean cannot resolve anything under ~22 %, so the 2.3x
#   between draft-mtp and everything else is solid and the small differences
#   in the table are not. Do not read a 1 t/s gap here as a finding.
#
#   All of the speed is draft-mtp's, and so is all of the defect. ngram-mod
#   neither costs nor buys anything HERE — but the prompt used had no
#   repetition in it, and repetition is the only thing an n-gram drafter can
#   exploit. The 87 t/s for warm multi-turn edits above came from exactly the
#   workload this measurement lacks, so ngram-mod stays: the measurement that
#   would justify dropping it has not been made.
# --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false}: thinking OFF is the
#   SERVER default; modes are selected per request by cc-gateway via model
#   name (KWARGS_BY_MODEL: qwen38=nothink, qwen38-think=low,
#   qwen38-deep=medium). Measured: a mode switch keeps the prompt cache
#   100 % warm. The JSON stands WITHOUT quotes on purpose - systemd and
#   bench/run.py both split on whitespace and pass quotes through as data.
# -cram 32768 = 32 GiB of RAM prompt cache, and it is RIGHT here — but it was
# never computed, only written, and then copied into every profile that came
# after. In Qwen3.8-Flash-Next the same line means 140 GiB on a 125 GiB
# machine. tests/test_models.py now checks each profile against its own
# weights, which is the thing that was missing rather than the number.
#
# The arithmetic for THIS profile. It is no longer written down here and
# checked by hand — setup/lib/budget.py computes it, the unit runs it as
# ExecStartPre, and `python3 setup/lib/budget.py --profile qwen38` prints it:
#
#     weights (the .gguf AND the mmproj)        17.6 GiB
#     KV at -c 204800, 74.3 KiB/token           14.5
#     compute buffers and loader, floor          6.0
#                                              ------
#                                               38.1   pinned in GTT
#     RAM prompt cache, worst case              32.0
#                                              ------
#                                               70.1   resident
#     host share                                12.0
#                                              ------
#                                               82.1 of 124.9
#
# The version of this table that stood here until 27.08. said "16.7 -> 31.2 in
# GTT (35.3 observed)" and left the 4.1 GiB gap unexplained. It is the mmproj
# (0.9, which the .gguf figure omits) and the compute buffers (3.2). Measured
# the same day: mem_info_gtt_used reads 35.6 with the desktop counted in it.
#
# The buffer line was `10 % of weights+KV` = 3.2 GiB until later that day, when
# nine recorded measurements showed the term is roughly CONSTANT at 3.1-4.6 and
# that the percentage under-predicted all nine. It is a floor above the worst
# of them now, so this profile predicts 38.1 against an observed 35.6 —
# conservative by 2.5 GiB, which is the direction a guard is allowed to err in.
# check.sh re-checks it after every start rather than leaving it as a sentence
# in a comment.
#
# What 32 GiB buys, at 74.3 KiB/token: ~32 typical prefixes of this project,
# ~10 contexts the size of Claude Code's tool head, or 2.2 full windows. The
# twelve prefixes actually on disk come to 7.74 GiB together and eleven of
# them have been restored at least once, so the cache earns its keep — it is
# provisioned about 4x above observed use, which is headroom rather than
# waste at this footprint.
# --- reasoning modes ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rule and the vocabulary: setup/claude/modes.py. The two lines below are
# GENERATED — `python3 bench/suites/effort-vocabulary.py --profile qwen38` —
# and everything model-specific about them is the measurement that follows.
#
# MEASURED 28.08.2026. This template renders three levels — low, medium and
# xhigh — and aliases `high` to xhigh itself. `max` and `none` RAISE, an HTTP
# 500 at the server, so max is sent as xhigh: the highest thing this template
# actually has.
#
# high, xhigh and max therefore all SEND xhigh, and that is deliberate. They
# render identically (sha 546e0307), so sending the consumer's own word would
# make three different chat_template_kwargs for one prompt — and since the
# kwargs entered the prefix id, three cache keys and two wasted prefills.
#
# `on+` sets BOTH knobs, and it is not decoration: this template gates the
# effort block on enable_thinking, and LLAMA_ARGS below switches that off. A
# mode sending only the level renders identically to sending nothing (measured:
# 1ad7792b either way) while carrying its own prefix id.
#
# No `none` mode: the bare alias already means "what the command line says",
# and that is thinking off. A `none` here would render 1ad7792b too — the same
# prompt under a second cache key. An unoffered name falls through to the bare
# alias and renders the same thing anyway, so leaving it out costs nothing.
MODES=low:on+low  medium:on+medium  high:on+high  xhigh:on+high  max:on+high
#
# TEMPLATE_LEVELS is the measurement, not a policy: what this template renders.
# It exists so a mode declaring something else — `max:on+max` looks
# symmetrical and is a 500 — is refused at load instead of once per request.
TEMPLATE_LEVELS=low  medium  high  xhigh
#
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias qwen38 -m @MODELS@/Qwen3.8-27B-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf --mmproj @MODELS@/mmproj-F16.gguf \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -ub 2048 -b 2048 -c 204800 -np 1 -cram 32768 \
  --slot-save-path @HOME@/.cache/llama-slots \
  --spec-type draft-mtp,ngram-mod --spec-draft-n-max 12 --spec-ngram-mod-n-min 24 \
  --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false} \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080

# Backend: ROCm — with MTP/ngram speculation Vulkan halves the decode
# (measured 24.08., vulkan-medium-spec vs rocm-medium-spec: 96.9 vs 51.7 s
# median). Read by llama-user@.service via
# ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'exec "$HOME/$LLAMA_BIN" $LLAMA_ARGS'.
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-rocm-patched/bin/llama-server
